      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are other reasons this is done as well, but it was getting too wordy to describe them all in a check-in comment.\n\nIf ADC is not disabled, light continues to be sampled after the irq.  If we disabled adc to set again/atime, then set adc again, we screw up the sample timing.\n\nFor instance, if frequency is 1Hz, and request comes on each second, the samples returned will be guaranteed to be 1 second apart (after writing that, I realize this statement is not 100% true, not guaranteed to be exactly 1 second apart, but will deal with that).  The sampling time is based on atime, which changes with this mechanism (but maximum sample time is 712ms, we have maximum frequency for the sensor at 1Hz.  Likewise, the time the light was sampled would also vary based on atime setting.\n\nAlso, if we only disable interrupt after irq fires and only re-enable interrupt when read() is called, we will likely interrupt immediately in many cases as a sample will have been gathered over the last second since ADC and oscillator was left on.  This environment would create a race condition in the case when you\u0027re reading the last value generated by the chip, but its sampling cycle finishes and it updates the light data registers with new values (once ADC and oscillator are shut off, light data registers can\u0027t be read, which is why the irq handler drops into an IDLE mode first, then into a DISABLE after reading registers).  There may be a way to get past such a race condition if chip supports some sort of \"hold-off\" mutex that could be used when reading data, but given the other possible issues with the approach, and that I don\u0027t see an advantage to leaving the sensor running since max rate of an ALS sensor is 1 second, although that may be what we\u0027ll need to do if the issue I\u0027m about to explain turns out to be an actual issue.\n\nIn explaining that right now, I just realized a possible issue we\u0027re going to have with CTS tests and the fact that atime is adjustable (i.e. sensor frequency would not be constant with respect to when events are delivered since sample time is based on atime, and CTS will fail if delta between timestamp of samples does not match set frequency of the sensor).  I\u0027ll think about that some more and how I might address that (if needed), although I\u0027ve heard that the ALS CTS test is not that stringent, so I\u0027ll find out.  In any event, it\u0027s not an issue I would hold up this CL for.  \n\nI have discussed this approach with AMS, and they encourage the solution implemented in this CL.",
